<h2>Where the duty comes from</h2>
<p>Three layers combine. First, the statute: Section 482(2) BNSS permits the court to require the applicant&rsquo;s availability for interrogation. Second, the general notice power: Section 35(3) BNSS — the successor to Section 41A CrPC — lets the investigating officer require the appearance of a person whose arrest is not immediately necessary; an anticipatory bail applicant served with such a notice must honour it. Third, the order itself: courts routinely add tailored conditions — joining within a fixed number of days, appearing on specified weekdays, or appearing whenever called with twenty-four or forty-eight hours&rsquo; notice. The applicant&rsquo;s obligations are the sum of all three, and the order&rsquo;s specific terms control.</p>

<h2>What cooperation looks like in practice</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Appear on the notice, personally.</strong> Attendance through counsel does not discharge a direction for personal appearance. Arrive on time, sign the arrival and departure entries, and request an acknowledgment of attendance.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Answer, within constitutional limits.</strong> Respond to questions about facts; where an answer would be directly self-incriminating, the privilege can be invoked — politely and specifically, not as a blanket refusal to engage.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Produce what is lawfully demanded.</strong> Documents, account statements, devices against seizure memos, specimen signatures where directed. Supplying documents in organised, indexed form is itself evidence of cooperation when the matter returns to court.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Stay reachable and still.</strong> Keep the disclosed address and mobile number live, seek leave before foreign travel, and never contact the complainant or witnesses — the no-inducement condition is breached by &ldquo;settlement feelers&rdquo; sent through relatives as much as by threats.</div>
</div>

<h2>The two failure modes</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Consequence</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Non-appearance despite notices; medical certificates produced serially; appearance only through counsel</td><td>Interim protection vacated or final relief refused; after grant, cancellation application by the State with a strong factual foundation.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Appearance with obstruction — refusal to produce devices or documents lawfully demanded, evasive answers on verifiable facts, coaching of witnesses</td><td>Recorded in the status report as non-cooperation; colours the court&rsquo;s discretion decisively, because the grant was premised on investigation remaining unimpeded.</td></tr>
</table>
<p>It bears emphasis that the converse is equally established: the mere fact that an applicant did not confess, or that the investigation did not progress to the officer&rsquo;s satisfaction, is not non-cooperation. The condition secures the applicant&rsquo;s availability and the investigation&rsquo;s integrity; it does not conscript him as a prosecution witness against himself.</p>

<h2>A compliance file wins cases</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Maintain a running file: every notice received, every appearance made (with date, time in and time out), every document produced with its covering index, every device handed over with its memo.</p>
  <p>Reply to notices in writing when a date must be adjusted, proposing an alternative within days — silence followed by absence is how good cases turn bad.</p>
  <p>If the officer&rsquo;s demands exceed lawful bounds — roving seizure of family members&rsquo; devices, pressure for statements late at night — comply where lawful, object on record where not, and move the court for clarification rather than self-adjudicating the dispute by staying away.</p>
  <p>At the final hearing, the compliance file converts the cooperation condition from the prosecution&rsquo;s talking point into the defence&rsquo;s exhibit.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer is general information on anticipatory bail practice, not advice for any particular case. The specific conditions in the order governing an applicant always take precedence, and should be read closely with counsel on the day they are passed.</div>
